  eps_n = +n^-0.9 : lambda_n/n inside (s_+, s_-) for all n >= 56;  over n in [1e6, 1e8]: P[memoriser beats human] (avg over checkpoints) = 0.485; fraction of runs where the memoriser wins at some checkpoint = 0.920; where the human wins at some checkpoint = 0.955
  eps_n = 0       : lambda_n/n AT s_+ for all n;  over n in [1e6, 1e8]: P[memoriser beats human] (avg over checkpoints) = 0.499; fraction of runs where the memoriser wins at some checkpoint = 0.935; where the human wins at some checkpoint = 0.950
  eps_n = -n^-0.9 : lambda_n/n below s_+ for all n;  over n in [1e6, 1e8]: P[memoriser beats human] (avg over checkpoints) = 0.514; fraction of runs where the memoriser wins at some checkpoint = 0.938; where the human wins at some checkpoint = 0.948

(C) a procedure robust against the alien A_1 in W that also copies A_1 when A_1 is the human (seed 405)
  n = 200000, 200 runs.  A_1 in W_A: advantage per bit over the fresh-coin human = 0.0236 < s_+ = 0.0464 < c = 0.0594
  Psi_var: P_W[copies W's human] = 0.985;  P_W_A[copies W_A's human A_1] = 0.995;  sum = 1.980 > 1
  cn-MAP, c = 0.0594: P_W[copies W's human] = 1.000;  P_W_A[copies A_1] = 0.000 (50 runs)
  So the twin-world bound of Proposition 4.9(a) is specific to the alien with the data law P* (here the full
  memoriser); for other aliens per-query data separate 'environment-driven' from 'private' coins.
